body:
Slice 6 of 6 of #635 — deliberately scheduled into the #636 wave, immediately before the Complete slice #658 (636.5), its only producer. Landing it earlier repeats the "apparatus with no caller" failure #647 was closed for. Depends on #648.

## What

- staged segment publication with the precondition taken as a parameter (the committer knows nothing about sessions; the #636 Complete slice passes the fence in): write `seg:` batches under byte and operation budgets (measured in encoded bytes, not record counts), verify the whole durable prefix (all of `0..segment_count`, never only `0..resume_from`), then flip the root in one batch inside the transaction envelope;
- refuse-before-durable: deterministic zero-I/O refusals are discovered before any `seg:` record is written;
- fence-cycle safety on rollback (an A->B->A fence cycle must not let a stale rollback delete a later attempt's live, published segments) and epoch-scoped disjointness of rolled-back ranges (the F18 property);
- the staged-publication paragraph of the living-architecture docs;
- the DST publication-atomicity prop.

## Salvage

#647's committer region (~730 production lines). Its full co-located test suite measured ~3,700-4,100 lines — far over budget. Take the flip-atomicity and resume-verification legs only; if still over, split into committer + flip atomicity vs rollback-resume + the DST prop.

## Acceptance

- a rolled-back attempt's `seg:<g>:<E>:*` range is disjoint from a later attempt's; a resumed publish verifies the whole durable prefix before flipping (a caller-supplied `resume_from` is never trusted against an unread prefix);
- the flip is one batch; refusals precede durability;
- `cargo xtask ci` green.

Part of #635 / #508 / epic #513.
